          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"ROXANE:\\nWell! Your own self at last triumphs over yourself,\\nAnd it is now for your soul alone that **I love you**!\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"At the siege of Arras, Roxane speaks to Christian, whom she has married and believes to be the author of the eloquent letters sent in his name. Having risked reaching the battlefield, she explains that those letters transformed her former attraction to his beauty into love for his inner self; she would now love him even if he were ugly. Christian knows that Cyrano supplied the words and realizes that Roxane unknowingly loves what is Cyrano’s spiritual and verbal self. This realization raises the stakes for both men shortly before Christian’s death and prevents the truth from being disclosed to Roxane for many years.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The line is a direct, emphatic avowal of Roxane’s present emotional state, with the added truth-conditional claim that her love now rests solely on the addressee’s soul rather than his appearance. Its exclamation and balanced wordplay heighten intensity, but intensity alone does not make the utterance a reflexive emotional discharge: Roxane articulates a considered distinction and explains how her love has changed. The declaration profoundly affects her marriage and the Cyrano-Christian triangle, yet it does not itself promise future conduct or institute a new commitment. Its dramatic irony is especially valuable. Roxane sincerely reports what she believes, while mistakenly attributing the letters’ eloquence—and thus the soul she loves—to Christian. The surrounding deception complicates the object of her love without changing the core semantic force of her statement into a lie, quotation, or indirect avowal.\"}"
        }
